    date: "2026-10-26",
    text: "The Shamonda virus is a vector-borne virus belonging to the genus Orthobunyavirus in the family Peribunyaviridae. It is part of the Simbu group of viruses, which also includes Schmallenberg virus which is known to cause congenital malformations and stillbirths in cattle and other domesticated species. The virus, first isolated in cattle in Nigeria in 1965 and since identified in parts of Africa, Asia, and Europe, is understood to spread through bites from Culicoides midges (\u201csmall, two-winged fly-like insects\u201d). Although recent reports have primarily concerned cattle, cases have also been reported in sheep, goats, horses, and even alpacas. Fever, diarrhoea, reduced milk production, and reproductive disorders such as abortions and foetal malformations are some of the signs reported in animals neurological signs are less common. Since these can often resemble other diseases, laboratory testing is needed to confirm infection. According to the World Organisation for Animal Health (WOAH), two different variants of the virus Shamonda virus Europe-1 (SHAV-EU1) and Shamonda virus Europe-2 (SHAV-EU2) have caused this year\u2019s outbreaks in Europe. Although its long-term impact and significance in Europe is still unclear, outbreaks caused by the Simbu group tend to occur in cycles as per the seasonal patterns of midges. The behaviour of the European variants of Shamonda, especially in cattle populations that are immunologically naive, may differ from that of the virus in endemic countries, WOAH noted. Although French authorities said that \u201cclinical, biological and epidemiological investigations are still ongoing\u201d, the case in Lyon was detected after a hospitalised leukemia patient in their sixties went blind and became confused before falling into a coma. After the doctors clinically ruled out known pathogens and other likely causes, they tested the patient\u2019s cerebrospinal fluid a clear, colourless, water-like fluid that surrounds, cushions, and protects the brain and spinal cord for genetic traces of any pathogen, only to find markers of the Shamonda virus. After the patient\u2019s death, the medical team managed to sequence the entire genome from samples of his brain it was later confirmed to be the SHAV-EU1 variant. At this point of the investigation, the patient is understood to have contracted the virus through a midge bite in eastern France, where the virus was circulating in animals during the spring and summer. Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus, director-general of the World Health Organization (WHO), said that the global health monitor was in contact with French authorities who are collecting information to better understand the source of infection in the first human patient, possible routes of transmission, and any potential risk to public health. \u201cThe patient was immunocompromised, which made them more vulnerable to infection and severe disease, and unfortunately died. According to WOAH, there are presently no commercially available vaccines against the Shamonda virus. WOAH, however, recommended a few measures to control and manage risks arising out of potential Culicoides vectors in order to decrease the virus\u2019s transmission. Some of the measures are physical barriers such as fine mesh screens and double-door entry-exit systems, reducing or eliminating vector-breeding sites, vector surveillance and control around buildings, use of insect repellents during animal transport, and transporting animals during periods of low vector activity. The recent Covid-19 pandemic highlighted the grave risks and consequences presented by zoonotic diseases infections caused by viruses or pathogens spilling over from animal species into humans, and the possible impact in the case of transmission between human hosts. According to Marc-Alain Widdowson, who leads the High Threat Pathogens team in WHO\u2019s Health Emergencies Programme, new potential transmission routes must be seriously considered and efforts needed to be directed towards preventing diseases from becoming endemic to new areas."
