    date: "2026-10-26",
    text: "The main Uttarakhand Assembly complex is located in Dehradun. A second Assembly complex at Bhararisain, near Gairsain, was built following a movement for a capital in the mountains, a demand that predates Uttarakhand\u2019s formation in 2000. Dehradun, located in the plains, was designated the temporary capital when the new state was carved out of Uttar-Pradesh. Gairsain, a tehsil in Chamoli district, lies in the foothills of the Dudhatoli mountain-range, near the geographical divide between the Garhwal and Kumaon regions. According to the Census-2011, Gairsain tehsil had a population of 61,996. The demand to establish the capital of a separate hill state at Gairsain emerged decades before Uttarakhand was formed. Freedom fighter Veer Chandra Singh Garhwali, remembered for his role in the 1930 Peshawar incident in which soldiers of the Garhwal Rifles refused to fire on unarmed protesters, advocated a capital in the hills in the 1960s. When Uttarakhand was formed on November-9, 2000, Dehradun was designated the temporary capital, rather than Gairsain. The absence of adequate infrastructure, along with limited rail and air connectivity, was among the reasons cited for choosing Dehradun. The city already had an established administrative infrastructure and was considerably better connected to the national capital and other parts of the country. The idea was subsequently taken up by regional political organisations campaigning for a separate Uttarakhand. Successive governments have since held occasional Assembly sessions in Gairsain, but Dehradun has continued to house the principal administrative machinery. The demand gained more political weight in 1994, when the Ramashanker Kaushik committee, constituted by the Uttar Pradesh government to examine the creation of a separate hill state, recommended Gairsain as the most suitable location for its capital, because of its location between the Garhwal and Kumaon regions. The recommendation reflected one of the central arguments behind the statehood movement a government based in the hills would be more accessible to the people living there and better placed to address their concerns. The movement for a separate state was also driven by demands for political representation, employment and development. At the time, the Uttarakhand region had 22 seats in the Uttar Pradesh Assembly, despite having a population comparable to Himachal Pradesh, which had a substantially larger legislature. The statehood campaigners argued that decisions taken from the plains often failed to account for the geography and needs of the mountainous region, where access to healthcare, education, roads and government services remained difficult. The Uttarakhand Mahila Manch, which played a significant role in the statehood movement, was among the organisations that demanded Gairsain as the capital. The decision, however, left unresolved the question of a permanent capital. In 2001, the state government constituted a commission headed by retired judge Virender Dixit to examine possible locations for a permanent capital. The commission submitted its report in 2009, recommending Dehradun as the more suitable location, citing factors including its distance from the national capital, concentration of population and relative safety from natural calamities. The question of a permanent capital has consequently remained a recurring political issue, particularly during Assembly elections. The Bhararisain Assembly complex emerged from efforts to give the Gairsain demand a physical and institutional presence. In November 2012, then Chief Minister Vijay Bahuguna, who was with the Congress, held a Cabinet meeting in Gairsain at the local block office. The meeting revived discussion about shifting the capital to the town and was accompanied by an announcement that Gairsain would host at least one Assembly session each year. Bahuguna laid the foundation stone of a permanent Vidhan Sabha building at Bhararisain in January 2013. In June 2014, the temporary Assembly was arranged as a 100-foot-by 80-foot tent."
