    id: "ar-6756",
    testNumber: 136,
    title: "Test #136: Farmers Get Third of Veggies Retail Price",
    language: "english",
    category: "up_police_co",
    duration: 15,
    wordCount: 560,
    difficulty: "medium",
    date: "2024-10-09",
    text: "Indian farmers are getting only about one-third of the final selling price of fruits and vegetables, while a majority share is skimmed away by wholesalers and retailers, a series of research papers published by RBI on food inflation has showed. This is in contrast to other sectors, such as dairy, where farmers receive around 70% of the final price. Egg producers seem to be best placed, getting 75% of the final price, while for poultry meat, farmers and aggregators together account for 56% of the price. While consumers fret about tomato, onion and potato prices at least twice a year as prices soar on seasonal factors such as excess or deficient rains or high or low temperatures, it is not as farmers are benefiting. In the export market, while the share for mangoes increases, the share for grapes drops, even though the overall price is higher. The study, co-authored by agriculture economist Ashok Gulati, also concludes that forecasting price spikes is possible through a balance sheet approach. The papers also suggest various ways in which policymakers can smooth out these price spikes. To prevent price spikes in TOP, the study recommended expanding private mandis, leveraging e-NAM, promoting farmer collectives, and relaunching futures trading. It also suggests building more cold storage facilities, promoting solar-powered storage, increasing processing capacity, and raising consumer awareness on processed TOP products. Additionally, improving productivity through better crop varieties and polyhouse tomato farming has been recommended to ensure stable supply and prices. By forecasting the likelihood of a price spike, the research indicates that prices can be stabilised by short-term measures like adjusting trade policies to manage imports and exports based on supply-demand conditions. In the long term, it recommends improving productivity through innovation, better storage, and collective farming to increase farmers earnings. Enhancing the efficiency of the marketing system, integrating digital platforms like e-NAM, and improving processing technologies are also key to ensuring better price stability and transparency. For milk, poultry meat, and egg prices, it also recommends creating a feed bank to store and supply affordable fodder, utilising barren lands for grass cultivation, and promoting artificial insemination and disease control to increase livestock productivity. For fruits, similar recommendations include improving the supply chain with better storage and transport, promoting different fruit varieties, offering crop insurance, expanding processing and exports, adjusting import duties to match demand, and using digital tools to track supply and reduce price swings.\n\nIn the operational architecture of Uttar Pradesh Police, technological adaptation has transformed administrative transparency and tactical responsiveness. The integration of modern analytical dashboards enables precinct supervisors to track incident lifecycles from emergency phone reporting through forensic evidence collection and final court submission. Computer operators stationed at district control centers must process voluminous textual transmissions including daily case diaries, special surveillance bulletins, and statutory notices under stringent time constraints.\n\nTypographical precision remains foundational to digital governance within law enforcement agencies. Any transcription oversight or inadvertent omission during records entry can compromise the legal validity of prosecutorial documentation or cause administrative delays during judicial hearings. Candidates preparing for state recruitment examinations are therefore evaluated not merely on gross finger velocity across the keyboard, but fundamentally on sustained concentration, typographical discipline, and the ability to maintain above eighty-five percent accuracy throughout the full fifteen-minute testing interval. Consistent keyboard drills and systematic text auditing cultivate the motor memory necessary for high-speed administrative documentation."
