    id: "ar-5932",
    testNumber: 145,
    title: "Test #145: The Country could be the Next Chess Superpower",
    language: "english",
    category: "up_police_co",
    duration: 15,
    wordCount: 566,
    difficulty: "medium",
    date: "2024-09-25",
    text: "There was good reason for Indians on social media to borrow the British soccer slogan during the Chess Olympiad in Budapest. Chess is believed to have originated in India and the Indian teams did bring trophies home, scoring a huge double triumph. Both the Open and Women teams won gold, with four players also winning individual golds. Since the demise of Soviet Union, this double has been achieved only once, by China in 2018. India did it very convincingly, winning both segments with something to spare. For the Open team, Gukesh and Arjun Erigaisi delivered amazing performances winning individual golds, while Praggnanandhaa, Vidit Gujrathi and Harikrishna produced solid support. For the women, Divya Deshmukh and Vantika Agrawal won individual golds, while Harika, Tania Sachdev and Vaishali played supporting roles. The 18-year-old Gukesh is due to play a world title match in Nov in Singapore, against reigning world champion, Ding Liren of China. Interestingly, Ding did not play in the key India-China encounter, opting to avoid a prequel to that duel. Instead, Gukesh won a hair-raising encounter against WeiYi, finding a sequence of very difficult moves with seconds to spare. Gukesh scored eight wins and two draws in 10 games. The 21-year-old Arjun Erigaisi is now ranked World No 3, after an equally compelling performance. For the women, 18-year-old Divya Deshmukh logged 9.5 points in 11 games, dropping three draws. She moved into the World Top 10 and closer to the Grandmaster title. Debutant Vantika Agrawal also took individual gold with 7.5 points from 9 games. Sibling grandmasters Vaishali (23) and Pragg (19) are also pretty young, making youth a leitmotif of Indian success. Yet, most of these players are veterans of the Indian teams that suffered the heartbreaking disappointment of winning only bronze medals at the Chennai Olympiad in 2022. The two intervening years have seen them all improving in terms of playing strength, and maybe, emotional stability. Gukesh indeed made a reference to Chennai, when he said he was looking to seek redemption since he blames himself for a loss in Chennai, which cost India gold. Youth was also a talking point in Chennai with Uzbek and Indian youngsters making their mark. Budapest indicates the youngsters have now clearly overtaken the veterans in what is increasingly a young person\u2019s sport. And this is where India could build upon its gains after this Olympiad. Obviously Gukesh will be working single-mindedly to put it across Ding in Singapore.\n\nIn the operational architecture of Uttar Pradesh Police, technological adaptation has transformed administrative transparency and tactical responsiveness. The integration of modern analytical dashboards enables precinct supervisors to track incident lifecycles from emergency phone reporting through forensic evidence collection and final court submission. Computer operators stationed at district control centers must process voluminous textual transmissions including daily case diaries, special surveillance bulletins, and statutory notices under stringent time constraints.\n\nTypographical precision remains foundational to digital governance within law enforcement agencies. Any transcription oversight or inadvertent omission during records entry can compromise the legal validity of prosecutorial documentation or cause administrative delays during judicial hearings. Candidates preparing for state recruitment examinations are therefore evaluated not merely on gross finger velocity across the keyboard, but fundamentally on sustained concentration, typographical discipline, and the ability to maintain above eighty-five percent accuracy throughout the full fifteen-minute testing interval. Consistent keyboard drills and systematic text auditing cultivate the motor memory necessary for high-speed administrative documentation."
